. Reloads all the changes

  sudo systemctl --system

. it’s a single command. It installs the prerequisite packages for adding external apt repositories, 
  which you’ll need next for containerd and Kubernetes:

curl: downloads files and GPG keys from URLs.
gnupg2: handles GPG keys used to verify repository signatures.
software-properties-common: provides tools like add-apt-repository.
apt-transport-https: lets apt fetch packages over HTTPS (already built into modern apt, so this is mostly a harmless placeholder).
ca-certificates: lets the system trust HTTPS certificates.

Commands is: 

Command - sudo apt install -y curl gnupg2 software-properties-common apt-transport-https ca-certificates

Note:
(container runtime) for k8s cluster, first install its dependencies

Command - sudo curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmour -o /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/docker.gpg

Note: 
Enabling  dearmour repostory and
The command wraps in the terminal (ubun / tu/gpg), but it’s a single command.
It downloads Docker’s GPG signing key and saves it in a format apt can use, so your system can verify packages from the Docker repository
(which is where containerd comes from).

curl -fsSL: fails on errors, runs silently, follows redirects, and shows errors only if something goes wrong.
gpg --dearmour -o ...: converts the key from text (ASCII-armored) to binary and writes it to /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/docker.gpg

Command - sudo add-apt-repository "deb [arch=amd64] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable"

Note:

The command wraps in the terminal (https://down / load.docker.com), but it’s a single command. 
It adds Docker’s official apt repository, which is where containerd.io comes from. This is the step I mentioned at the end of my last reply.

[arch=amd64]: restricts the repo to 64-bit x86 packages.
$(lsb_release -cs): expands to your Ubuntu release codename (for example jammy or focal), so apt pulls the matching packages.
stable: selects Docker’s stable release channel.



