 . Configure Container so that it start using systemd as c group:

  Command: 
  containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml >/dev/null 2>&1
  sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup \= false/SystemdCgroup \= true/g' /etc/containerd/config.toml

 Note: The command wraps in the terminal (/etc/containerd/c / onfig.toml), but it’s a single command.
 It’s the first line of the containerd setup I mentioned in my last reply.
 containerd config default: prints containerd’s default configuration to the terminal.
 sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml: writes that output to the config file with root permissions (needed because /etc/containerd/ is protected).
 >/dev/null 2>&1: silences tee’s normal echo of the file contents, so your terminal stays clean.

